.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_CREATE 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_create \- create a ranged memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_r_mempool *ccol_r_mempool_create(
    uint8_t smallest_size_power_of_two,
    uint8_t largest_size_power_of_two,
    uint8_t number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two,
    ccol_r_memory_fallback_policy_t fb_policy,
    bool single_threaded,
    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
    char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a ranged memory pool that manages many fixed-size pools. The
power-of-2 sizes organize those pools. The ranged pool selects the right
sub-pool for each allocation request.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
smallest_size_power_of_two              Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two               Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
fb_policy                               Fallback policy for an empty pool
single_threaded                         If true, do not lock
mmgmt_procs                             Custom memory management, or NULL
err                                     Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the new ranged memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Each sub-pool has half the elements of the size before it
The smallest size that the pool accepts is 16 bytes (power = 4)
Example: smallest=4 (16 bytes), largest=6 (64 bytes), count=8 (256 elems)
  This creates three pools: 16 bytes (256 elems), 32 bytes (128), and
  64 bytes (64)
Fallback policies:
  - ccol_fallback_disabled: Tries each larger pool in sequence. Returns
    NULL when all of them are empty too
  - ccol_fallback_at_first_exhaustion: When the pool that fits the
    request has no free entries, that pool gives the memory from the
    heap. The request never goes to a larger pool
  - ccol_fallback_at_last_exhaustion: Tries each larger pool in
    sequence. Uses the heap only when all the pools that can serve the
    request are empty
Each sub-pool calls the functions of mmgmt_procs for its dynamic
fallback entries, and for its own bookkeeping of them, in the middle of
an update of its own state, with its lock held when it has one. Those
functions must therefore not call back into this same ranged pool: with
a lock the call can deadlock, and without one it corrupts that state. An
allocator built on a pool must draw from a different pool

Sizing for performance:

Each sub-pool has its own size. The 8-element floor that
ccol_mempool_create(3) describes therefore applies to each tier, and not
to the pool as a whole. Each tier holds half the elements of the tier
below it. The largest few element sizes therefore have the fewest
entries, and they are the first to fall under that floor. A tier with
fewer than 8 elements takes its lock on every allocation and every free.

A higher number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two lifts every tier at
once. It is the simplest way to keep the tiers with large elements above
the floor. A narrower range between smallest_size_power_of_two and
largest_size_power_of_two has the same effect. It produces fewer tiers
to divide the elements between.

Every tier that receives a cache allocates a reserve of its own. The
memory of a ranged pool can therefore exceed the sum of its nominal tier
sizes by up to half. It exceeds that sum by proportionally less for a
tier large enough for its caches to reach maximum depth. The library
allocates that memory when it creates the pool, so nothing grows at run
time. ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity() reports the nominal count of a
tier. A tier moves up to a larger tier, or falls back, once that count
runs out. There is one exception, and it is the same one that
ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3) describes. An entry leaves the cache of a
thread with no lock held, and so with no count to consult. Concurrent
callers can therefore hold a few entries more than the nominal count of
a tier at one time, for a short time. They never hold more than that
count plus the reserve of the tier.

The best range, tier count and single_threaded setting depend on the
size distribution of the allocations of the program. No default can
predict that distribution. Measure rather than assume. "make bench" in
the source tree runs these pools beside the system allocator, on the
current machine.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when single_threaded is false. Each sub-pool carries its own
lock. Allocations for different size classes therefore never contend
with one another. Inside a tier large enough to keep a cache for each
thread, the common path takes no lock at all.

An entry in the cache of a thread returns to its tier when that thread
exits. A destroy of the pool does not return such an entry. It frees the
whole block of each tier, those entries included. It also cuts every
thread cache loose without a read of it. The thread that owns a cache
writes its contents with no synchronization. A read of those contents
from the thread that destroys the pool is therefore a data race. The small allocation
of a cache is a separate matter. The library frees it at the next
allocation of its owning thread from any pool, at the exit of that
thread, or at process exit. The pool can force none of these three. A
program that needs its allocator to see every free should join such
threads before it destroys the pool.
.fi
